  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSoutherly flow will weaken a bit late this afternoon into Friday as \na northern Plains low lifts ENE through Canada, exerting less \ninfluence on the pressure gradient over central IL. Highs today will \ncontinue the warming trend, with upper 60s expected. Winds from the \nsouth will be a bit breezy early in the day with around 10 mph winds \nand gusts 15-20, diminishing during the afternoon and evening. \n\nBy Friday, the next low pressure system emerging into the northern \nPlains will increase southerly flow and the pressure gradient over \ncentral IL again, promoting around 10-15 mph southerly winds and \ngusts 15-20 mph again both Friday and Saturday. Highs will reach the \nupper 60s to around 70 both Friday and Saturday. The cold front \ntrailing this low looks to push through central IL from Sunday \nmorning through Sunday evening, bringing scattered showers and a few \nthunderstorms ahead of it, mainly late Saturday night through Sunday \nafternoon. Chances for precipitation are now 70 to 80 percent, \nhowever precipitation amounts still look to be generally under a \nquarter inch offering no meaningful drought relief overall. \nNevertheless, ensemble probabilities for over an inch are not \nnegligible, with typical values around 5 percent, suggesting a few \nlocally heavier amounts over an inch will be possible. Joint \nprobability for MUCAPE > 100 J/kg and deep layer shear > 30 kts from \n12Z Ensembles are around 20 percent, suggesting a few thunderstorms \nwill be possible as a moist corridor with dewpoints in the 50s sets \nup ahead of the front. Temperatures will begin to trend down Sunday \nas the cold front, cloud cover, and showers affect the area, with \nhighs ranging from mid 50s northwest of the IL River to lower 60s \neast of I-57.\n\nCold, dry northerly flow with high pressure moving into the Great \nLakes region should set up early next week, bringing highs in the \n40s and lows in the 20s. By midweek, models are developing a theme \nof a central U.S. trough, however significant discrepancies on the \ndetails remain. Precipitation can't be ruled out by Wednesday, \nhowever there is no indication at this point that there will be a \nsignificant precipitation event for central IL.",
